  "entendaTitle": "Understand the process",
  "entendaLede": "Short chapters on how the result leaves the voting machine and reaches you. Every statement cites an official TSE source.",
  "entendaCh1Title": "The path of a vote",
  "entendaCh1Summary": "From the voting machine to the posted report, from transmission to publication.",
  "entendaCh2Title": "The ballot box report",
  "entendaCh2Summary": "What the Boletim de Urna (BU) contains, why it is public and how to check it.",
  "entendaCh3Title": "The voting machine files",
  "entendaCh3Summary": "BU, RDV and log: what each one is for.",
  "entendaCh4Title": "Signatures and hashes",
  "entendaCh4Summary": "What this app's checks actually verify.",
  "entendaCh5Title": "Security protocols",
  "entendaCh5Summary": "Public tests, ceremonies and code inspection.",
  "entendaCh6Title": "October 4, 2026",
  "entendaCh6Summary": "What stopped, what did not, and how the replay shows it.",
  "entendaCh7Title": "What this app proves",
  "entendaCh7Summary": "And, just as clearly, what it does not prove.",
  "entendaChapterLabel": "Chapter {number}",
  "entendaPausePara1": "On the night of October 4, 2026, at about 19:14 (Brasília time), the national (\"Brasil\") view of the results for President stopped updating, when 64.81% of the data had been published. Generation of those files was restored at 20:04, about 50 minutes later.",
  "entendaPausePara2": "According to TSE, a data-processing contention, after a rise in data volume, stalled the module that generates the publication files for the \"Brasil\" view. The state view was not affected: each state's files, which also carry the votes for President, kept being published without interruption.",
  "entendaPauseCallout": "Counting (apuração) is done by each voting machine. Totalization (totalização) is the sum of those counts. Publication (divulgação) is the release on the internet. What stopped was the national publication.",
  "entendaPausePara3": "Anyone adding up the per-state (UF) files during the pause saw the count keep going. That is what the Count screen shows in the replay, with the pause at the same time. How the states moved during the pause is a synthetic curve: Urna Aberta kept only that night's final files.",
  "entendaSources": "Sources",
  "entendaSourceDivulgacao": "TSE · Informações técnicas sobre a divulgação de resultados (technical information on results publication)",
  "entendaCh1Para1": "Voting runs from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Brasília time. A polling section (seção eleitoral) closes only after everyone who received a number has voted. The electronic voting machine (urna eletrônica) then counts the section's votes and prints the ballot box tally report (Boletim de Urna, BU).",
  "entendaCh1Para2": "The presiding officer signs the copies of the BU together with the other poll workers and any party monitors present, and posts a signed copy where everyone can see it. The BU, the digital vote record and the machine's other files are created and digitally signed.",
  "entendaCh1Para3": "The voting machine has no internet or network connection. The results media, a removable device holding the voting data, is taken out of the machine and delivered to the Electoral Justice, or the files are sent from the polling place itself through JE-Connect, over a private network. Before adding anything up, the Electoral Justice systems check each file's digital signature and integrity.",
  "entendaCh1Callout": "Apuração (vote counting) is the count each machine makes. Totalização (totalization) adds up the counts of every machine in Brazil and abroad. Divulgação (publication of results) is the last step: publishing on the Resultados portal.",
  "entendaCh1Para4": "The three steps run in sequence, and results are published as the reports arrive. That is why a pause in publication, like the one on October 4, stops neither the counting nor the totalization (chapter 6).",
  "entendaCh2Para1": "The Boletim de Urna is the printed result of one polling section. It identifies the municipality, the electoral zone, the section and the machine, and lists the number of eligible voters and of voters who turned out, the votes for each candidate and each party, blank and null votes, and the grand total.",
  "entendaCh2Para2": "Five mandatory copies are printed, plus up to five more. One is posted at the polling place, another stays with the presiding officer, and party monitors may receive a copy.",
  "entendaCh2Para3": "The printed BU carries QR codes with the voting data. Any citizen or oversight entity can read them with the Boletim na Mão or e-Título apps and compare them with the result TSE publishes for the same section.",
  "entendaCh2Para4": "TSE publishes online the reports sent for totalization. The files mirror the printed reports, without any processing by the totalization system. This is the file the My section screen fetches for your section.",
  "entendaCh3Para1": "Besides the report, each machine records other files. TSE makes the ballot box report, the digital vote record (Registro Digital do Voto, RDV) and the machine log available on the Resultados portal, for download, inspection and audit. The open-data portal carries the same files for every section, by state.",
  "entendaCh3Para2": "The RDV is the file where votes are recorded, separated by office and written to random positions. There is no way to link a vote in the RDV to a voter. The BU is generated from it, and the RDV lets parties recount the machine's votes at any time.",
  "entendaCh3Para3": "The log is the chronological record of the main operations of the machine's software: start and end of voting, reports printed, applications run, clock adjustments and contingency procedures.",
  "entendaCh3Para4": "For each section TSE also publishes an auxiliary file listing the hashes of the machine files it received and the status of each one (received, rejected, excluded or totalized). The My section screen uses this list.",
  "entendaCh4Para1": "A hash is a short string of characters computed from a file that identifies it unambiguously. However small the difference between two files, their hashes are completely different.",
  "entendaCh4Para2": "A digital signature uses a key pair: the private key, known only to the signer, and the public key, which can be given to everyone. Anyone holding the public key can check that the file came from the signer and was not changed.",
  "entendaCh4Para3": "The voting machine signs everything it outputs: the report, the RDV and the log. Published result files come in JSON and JWS pairs; the JWS carries the same data plus a TSE digital signature, using the EdDSA algorithm with an Ed25519 key. TSE's manual says the public key must not be taken from the file being checked.",
  "entendaCh4Callout": "Urna Aberta checks two things: TSE's signature on every result file (Verify screen) and the hash of your section's report against the list in the auxiliary file (My section screen).",
  "entendaCh4Para4": "Urna Aberta does not yet check the voting machine's own signature on the report. For that, scan the QR code on the printed report with the Boletim na Mão app.",
  "entendaCh5Para1": "The voting machine's source code is open to oversight entities one year before each election. For 2026 it opened on October 2, 2025. Those who may inspect include parties, federations and coalitions, the Bar Association (OAB), the Public Prosecutor's Office, the Public Defender's Office, Congress, the Comptroller General (CGU), the Federal Police and the Federal Court of Accounts (TCU).",
  "entendaCh5Para2": "In the Public Security Test, registered investigators try to attack the election systems. The edition for 2026 began on December 1, 2025, with 38 test plans. In the Confirmation Test, in May 2026, TSE concluded that no finding weakened the secrecy or integrity of the vote.",
  "entendaCh5Para3": "On September 4, 2026, at the Digital Signing and Sealing Ceremony, the systems were digitally signed by TSE and by representatives of the oversight entities, and the media were sealed and stored in TSE's vault room.",
  "entendaCh5Para4": "On election day, the Integrity Test uses machines drawn by lot the day before: between 8 a.m. and 5 p.m., paper ballots filled in by volunteers are typed in one by one, on camera and open to the public, and the machine's result is compared with the ballots. In 2026 there was also a version using volunteer voters' biometrics.",
  "entendaCh5Para5": "After totalization, oversight entities may request the machines' reports, RDVs and logs, and the records of the totalization systems.",
  "entendaCh7Para1": "Urna Aberta reads the same files TSE publishes and shows where each number comes from, with the file's code.",
  "entendaCh7ProvesTitle": "What it proves",
  "entendaCh7Para2": "That the result files were signed by TSE and not altered afterwards (EdDSA signature checked against the official key, on the server and, if you ask, on your device). That the independent sum of the states and abroad matches, or does not match, the national file. That the published report for your section is exactly the file whose hash appears in the auxiliary file's list.",
  "entendaCh7NotTitle": "What it does not prove",
  "entendaCh7Para3": "Anything about how the voting machine works, its software, or whether each vote was recorded as the voter intended: that is the job of the protocols in chapter 5. It does not yet check the machine's signature on the report, and it does not check that the published report matches the printed one: that comparison is yours to make, with the posted report or Boletim na Mão.",
  "entendaCh7Para4": "Parts marked as simulation, such as the states' progress in the October 4 replay, are synthetic curves, not official data.",
